From udf74d6fccec2ff7e at groups.google.com  Mon Jan 24 19:57:30 2011
From: udf74d6fccec2ff7e at groups.google.com (Richard Chandler)
Date: Mon, 24 Jan 2011 19:57:30 +0000
Subject: unmarked 0.9-0 released
Message-ID: <JZHTlBYh5rY.Td4Y-Sl9iRMJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/JZHTlBYh5rY/m/Td4Y-Sl9iRMJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello everyone,

I just uploaded a new version of unmarked <http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/unmarked/> to CRAN. The windows and mac binaries should be ready within a few days. Release notes can be found here: https://sites.google.com/site/unmarkedinfo/. There are two important changes that I want to highlight.

First, we added the remarkable model of Dail and Madsen (Biometrics early view <http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1541-0420.2010.01465.x/abstract>), which relaxes the closure assumption of the N-mixture model and allows for inference about the dynamics of open populations. See ?pcountOpen for more details.

Second, unmarked now depends on the Rcpp <http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/Rcpp/> and RcppArmadillo <http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/RcppArmadillo/> packages. Once the binaries are ready, you can get everything at once by typing:

install.packages("unmarked", dependencies=TRUE)

These packages make it easy to call C++ code from R. Currently we are only making slight use of these packages, but eventually we hope to see major speed improvements by writing all the likelihoods in C++.

Comments and feedback are welcome as always.

Richard

